While reading chapter 3, I made a lot of connections to what I learned in my media ethics class last spring. The Belmont Report's principle of "respect for persons" reminds me of the teachings of Immanuel Kant and the idea that we should never use another person as merely a means to an end. The rule of "protection of vulnerable populations" and the section about justice made me think of John Rawls and his philosophy related to justice, especially the "difference principle", which meant that any inequalities in society should benefit the most vulnerable and least privileged. The principle of beneficence relates to utilitarianism, and the idea of maximizing the ratio of benefits to risks is similar to the idea of doing what brings the greatest amount of happiness to the greatest number of people. The three principles established by the Belmont Report create a good framework for ethical questions when it comes to research. It's interesting to see these principles applied to real cases in research, such as how they apply to the Twitch dataset we use in this class, and to know that an Institutional Review Board's job is to review proposed studies for ethical concerns according to these principles.
